p.src:
Take the apparently structureless expression $S_{\mathrm{free}}
=\int d^4x\,d^2\theta\,d^2\bar\theta\;
\Phi^\dagger\Phi.$ The two factors depend on oppositely shifted coordinates: $\Phi=\Phi(x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta,\theta),
\qquad
\Phi^\dagger
=\Phi^\dagger(x-i\theta\sigma\bar\theta,\bar\theta).$ Inside the spacetime integral we may shift the dummy variable $x$. One shift can remove the Grassmann displacement from one factor, but then it doubles the relative displacement in the other. Taylor expansion in that nilpotent displacement terminates after finitely many terms.

